1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,800
Metadata can help agents, but the same strings can contain prompt injection,
secret requests, or executable markup.

2
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:11,720
MetaFence keeps raw content behind a visible human review boundary.

3
00:00:11,720 --> 00:00:19,400
The app starts with eight original synthetic records.
A deterministic scanner assigns clear, review, or quarantine states.

4
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:22,520
The normal human interface works in any modern browser.

5
00:00:22,520 --> 00:00:28,120
In a WebMCP browser, four narrowly scoped tools are registered
as a progressive enhancement.

6
00:00:28,120 --> 00:00:34,120
Here, the agent invoked stage_metadata_review with a quarantine filter
and a maximum of three records.

7
00:00:34,120 --> 00:00:36,400
The page visibly changed to the bounded queue.

8
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:44,480
The tool result contained IDs, lengths, risk labels, and reason codes,
but none of the raw untrusted descriptions.

9
00:00:44,480 --> 00:00:47,520
Next, the agent requested human review for one record.

10
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:51,520
The injected instruction appears only inside this clearly marked,
visible panel.

11
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:59,080
The agent's response says “raw text returned to agent: false”
and asks the person to inspect the panel.

12
00:00:59,080 --> 00:01:02,240
There is no approve, rewrite, or publish tool.

13
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:07,600
Finally, the agent staged a safe structural CSV.
The response reports four rows.

14
00:01:07,600 --> 00:01:10,800
Human confirmation required, and downloaded: false.

15
00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:13,360
A person must click the final control.

16
00:01:13,360 --> 00:01:16,120
Every action is recorded in the visible event log.

17
00:01:16,120 --> 00:01:20,100
MetaFence makes agents useful without making untrusted content authoritative.
